# Tutorial 01 — Dialogue Basics
# Character lines, narrator lines, and inline text formatting.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_01_dialogue
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Hi! I'm Lavender. This line you're reading is a {b}character dialogue line{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: And this line — with no name in the text box — is a {b}narrator line{/b}.
Lavender: Those two line types, plus a handful of formatting tags, are everything you need for Tutorial 01.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> Character dialogue lines -> section_lines
> Narrator lines -> section_narrator
> Text tags: bold, italic, color -> section_tags
> Text tags: wave and shake -> section_fx_tags
> Nesting tags together -> section_nesting
> Quick reference: dialogue syntax -> quick_reference
> Practice: a mini-scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Keep this pattern nearby while writing your first scenes:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character lavender "Lavender"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Character dialogue goes after the colon.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: Narration uses the narrator speaker ID.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Use \{b\}bold\{/b\}, \{i\}italic\{/i\}, and \{color=#4a9eff\}color\{/color\}.{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: declared speaker, readable line length, one emphasis tag only when it helps.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_lines
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Character Lines ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_lines
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A character line is a name, a colon, and the spoken text:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Hello, world!{/color}
Lavender: The name before the colon appears in the {b}name box{/b}. The text after it fills the {b}dialogue area{/b}.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The name must match a declared character ID. Characters are declared with {b}@character{/b}:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character lavender "Lavender"{/color}
narrator: The first word is the {b}script ID{/b}. The quoted part is the {b}display name{/b} the player sees.
Lavender: Multiple characters simply means multiple declarations:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character alex "Alex"{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@character narrator "Narrator"{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: One declaration per character, usually in a shared file. For this project, that's {b}scripts/definitions/characters.vns{/b}.
> Next: narrator lines -> section_narrator
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Narrator Lines ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_narrator
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: The special ID {b}narrator{/b} hides the name box entirely:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The hallway was empty.{/color}
narrator: The player sees only the text — no name label.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use narrator lines for three things:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Scene descriptions — {i}The sun dipped below the hills.{/i}
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Internal thoughts — {i}Something about this felt wrong.{/i}
narrator: {b}3.{/b} Stage directions — {i}She turned and walked away without another word.{/i}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Alternate between character lines and narrator lines to create rhythm. Characters speak, the narrator paints.
> Next: text tags -> section_tags
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Text Tags: Bold, Italic, Color ──────────────────────────────

@label section_tags
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Wrap words in tags to change how they render. Three essentials:
narrator: {b}Bold{/b} — \{b\}text\{/b\} — for emphasis and strong statements.
narrator: {i}Italic{/i} — \{i\}text\{/i\} — for thoughts, stress, and foreign words.
narrator: {color=#4a9eff}Color{/color} — \{color=#4a9eff\}text\{/color\} — for highlighting key words.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's each one in action.
Lavender: Bold: {b}I told you not to open that door.{/b}
Lavender: Italic: I {i}told{/i} you not to open that door.
Lavender: Color: I told you not to open that {color=#ff6666}door{/color}.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Same sentence, completely different weight each time. The tag chooses what the player notices first.
> Next: wave and shake -> section_fx_tags
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Text Tags: Wave and Shake ───────────────────────────────────

@label section_fx_tags
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Two animated tags add motion to text:
narrator: {wave}Wave{/wave} — \{wave\}text\{/wave\} — letters ripple gently. Good for dreamy or ethereal dialogue.
narrator: {shake}Shake{/shake} — \{shake\}text\{/shake\} — letters tremble. Good for fear, anger, or impact.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {wave}The world dissolved around her like watercolors in rain.{/wave}
Lavender: {shake}Get out. Now.{/shake}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Animated tags are powerful, so use them rarely. If every line shakes, none of them feel urgent.
> Next: nesting tags -> section_nesting
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Nesting Tags ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_nesting
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Tags nest by wrapping one inside another. Close the inner tag first:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}\{b\}\{color=#ff4444\}text\{/color\}\{/b\}{/color} → {b}{color=#ff4444}bold red text{/color}{/b}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}\{i\}\{wave\}text\{/wave\}\{/i\}{/color} → {i}{wave}italic wave{/wave}{/i}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Two practical rules:
narrator: {b}1.{/b} Keep nesting to two tags deep. Three is hard to read in script. Four is a mistake.
narrator: {b}2.{/b} Color the word, not the paragraph. {color=#4a9eff}One highlighted word{/color} guides the eye. A colored wall of text guides nothing.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's the full formatting toolkit. You now know every tag the engine supports.
> Practice: a mini-scene -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let's write a short scene together. You pick the scenario, and I'll show you how dialogue, narration, and tags combine.
> A character greets an old friend -> practice_greeting
> A character discovers something unsettling -> practice_discovery
> A character gives a speech -> practice_speech

@label practice_greeting
[hide lavender]
[wait 400]
narrator: The cafe door chimed. A familiar face appeared between the coats and umbrellas.
[show lavender left happy]
Lavender: {color=#66bb88}No way — it's really you!{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: I thought you moved away {i}years{/i} ago. Get over here!
narrator: She crossed the room in three quick steps, nearly knocking over a cup.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's what just happened in script:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[hide lavender]{/color} — cleared the screen.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The cafe door chimed...{/color} — set the scene with no name box.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender left happy]{/color} — she appears on the left, smiling.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: \{color=#66bb88\}No way...\{/color\}{/color} — colored text for warmth.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: ...{i}years{/i} ago.{/color} — italic for vocal emphasis.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: She crossed the room...{/color} — narrator describes the action.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_discovery
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: The drawer slid open with a groan. Inside: a photograph that shouldn't exist.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: That's... {b}that's not possible.{/b}
[wait 400]
narrator: {color=#8877bb}The date on the back read ten years from now.{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Here's the breakdown:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The drawer slid open...{/color} — narrator builds the moment.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender center emphasis]{/color} — expression changes to match the shock.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: ...\{b\}that's not possible.\{/b\}{/color} — bold for the gut reaction.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 400]{/color} — a pause to let it land.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: \{color=#8877bb\}The date...\{/color\}{/color} — muted color for an eerie reveal.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_speech
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: {b}Listen.{/b}
[wait 300]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: I know this isn't what anyone wanted to hear. But pretending won't help.
Lavender: We have {color=#ff8844}three days{/color}. That's it. So here's what we do.
narrator: The room was silent. Nobody argued.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: What happened there:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: \{b\}Listen.\{/b\}{/color} — bold on its own line for authority.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[wait 300]{/color} — a beat of silence after the command.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: ...\{color=#ff8844\}three days\{/color\}...{/color} — color highlights the key fact.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}narrator: The room was silent.{/color} — narrator sells the impact.
[jump practice_done]

@label practice_done
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Open this file in the editor and modify the scene you just played. Change a tag, swap a narrator line, try a different expression.
Lavender: The best way to learn VNS formatting is to break something small and watch what happens.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_01]
[flag tutorial_done_01]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_1]
